# Site-local Suricata rules for tzsp-monitor.
#
# Loaded after the Emerging Threats set, so anything here can also be used to
# suppress or complement it. Keep sids in the 9000000+ range: that block is
# reserved for local use and can never collide with a downloaded ruleset.

# Pipeline self-test. Fired by tzsp-decap/selftest.py, which is the only thing
# on a normal network that sends this string, so it is a safe "is the whole path
# from mirror to dashboard alive?" probe.
#
# priority:4 => alert severity 4, which sits below the responder's default
# block.max_severity of 2. Running the self-test therefore shows up on the
# dashboard but never puts anything on the router's address list.
alert ip any any -> any any ( \
    msg:"TZSP-MONITOR pipeline self-test"; \
    content:"TZSPMONITORSELFTEST"; \
    classtype:not-suspicious; \
    priority:4; \
    sid:9000001; rev:1;)

# Examples worth enabling once you know your own network -- left commented
# because either one is noisy on a network it was not measured against.
#
# Cleartext admin protocol crossing the perimeter:
# alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET 23 ( \
#     msg:"TZSP-MONITOR outbound telnet"; flow:to_server,established; \
#     classtype:policy-violation; priority:2; sid:9000002; rev:1;)
#
# SMB leaving the site (should never happen on a routed network):
# alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET 445 ( \
#     msg:"TZSP-MONITOR outbound SMB"; flow:to_server,established; \
#     classtype:policy-violation; priority:2; sid:9000003; rev:1;)
